ion wavefunction it certainly will  have momentum H Bar K what about if the  wavefunction is some kind of super  like this well then if we measure the  momentum of the particle it will either  be h-bar K 1 or H Bar K 2 the  probability of measuring either depends  on the size of these coefficients the  bigger the coefficients the more likely  the value is a similar thing works no  matter how big the superposition is  therefore if we're given a random wave  function and we want to know what the  momentum is most likely to be our first  task should be to write the function as  a superposition of exponential functions  and then look at the coefficients but  wait isn't this exactly what the Fourier  transform is good at doing remember that  if we want to decompose a function into  Exponential's the Fourier transform is  basically the thing that tells us how  much of each of the Exponential's we  have therefore if we look at the Fourier  transform of the function and see it's  very peaked at a point  it means the weights on the  Exponential's in that range are very  high but that means that the probability  of getting the corresponding Momentum's  is also high well then a more convenient  version of this graph is to multiply all  these K values by H bar because that  gives us the Momentum's using the de  Broglie hypothesis in this version of  the graph I can find out how likely a  certain range of Momentum's are by just  looking at how big the graphs are around  there but that means that this graph is  in fact the momentum wavefunction so all  we have to do to get from the position  wavefunction to the momentum one is to  take the Fourier transform and then  multiply all these K values by h-bar ok  so this is the link between momentum and  position in quantum mechanics but what's  so special about it  remember the uncertainty in a variable  is measured by the width of the  wavefunction or more precisely its  standard deviation the uncertainty  principle basically says that you can't  make the uncertainty in position and the  uncertainty in momentum arbitrarily  small at the same time well turns out  that this is just a property of the  Fourier transform say we have some  function  with uncertainty Delta X and its Fourier  transform has uncertainty Delta P let's  try and make the uncertainty in the  function smaller by squishing the  function to make its uncertainty go down  by a factor of D now surely the product  of uncertainty has gone down because  Delta X has been reduced  unfortunately the Fourier transform  thought sauce you see the Fourier  transform now spreads out so its  uncertainty goes up by a factor of D  therefore the product of uncertainty  doesn't decrease at all despite our best  efforts it's pretty straightforward to  show that this will happen no matter  what type of function we're using using  the formulas for the Fourier transform  but we'll look at some examples instead  this function is the hat function as you  can see its Fourier transform is pretty  crazy as we squish the hat though the  Fourier transform gets more and more  spread out a similar thing happens if  you start out with a Gaussian function  so you see it's this property that if a  function is lean then its Fourier  transform is wide and vice versa that  causes the position momentum uncertainty  let's just try one last pain time to  defy the uncertainty principle let's go  back and look at the function e to the  ikx if this is a particles position wave  function then what is its momentum  wavefunction well we know without a  doubt that the particle will have  momentum H Bar K so it's momentum  wavefunction is just a huge spike at  h-bar K in other words the uncertainty  in momentum is zero but if Delta P is  zero then Delta P times Delta X must be  zero so the uncertainty principle is  wrong right hmm not quite if we actually  calculate the position uncertainty we'll  find that it's infinite but this is very  weird because this means that if the  momentum is perfectly known and we try  and measure its position then the  particle is equally likely to turn up  anywhere in the universe I think this is  utterly ridiculous  so then is it actually  possible to get a particle into a state  where we know it's momentum perfectly  well last episode I told you that if we  measure a particles momentum it  collapses into a state of only having  that momentum so then my conclusion is  that it's impossible to measure the  momentum of a particle 100% precisely  even in theory because that would allow  the particle to turn up anywhere we can  use pretty similar logic to conclude  that we can't perfectly measure a  particles position either so from now on  when we talk about measuring a particle  in a certain spot or with a certain  momentum what we really mean is that it  approximately has that value well that's  it for our discussion of the uncertainty  principle for a bit I'll see you next  time for some more quantum mechanics  though
 

